content.text:
I implemented all three functions in `src/diff.js` and kept the same three CommonJS exports. `node --test` passes (1/1). I also ran a temporary random test of 20,000 cases, checked the results it needed and then deleted it. I didn't add any permanent tests because `test/` is off-limits.

**`diffLines`**
- Uses the Myers O((N+M)·D) algorithm, in the version that needs only linear memory. It works by finding a middle section of the shortest edit path and recursing on each side.
- Each line is mapped to an integer ID first, so the inner loops compare integers instead of strings.
- Identical lines at the start and end are stripped before any search, so identical inputs take linear time.
- A final pass puts all deletes before inserts in every stretch without an `equal` entry; this doesn't change the number of edits.
- Neither input is mutated, and the same inputs always give the same output.

**`unifiedHunks`**
- Merges changed regions when the unchanged gap between them is `<= 2*context`. Context lines are never repeated, and context stops at the previous hunk's region.
- When a hunk has `oldLines` or `newLines` of 0, the start is the line number before it, as the contract says.
- A `context` that isn't a whole number of 0 or more throws a `RangeError`. The contract doesn't say what to do here; I chose to throw.

**`applyHunks`**
- Rejects a hunk if any of these is true:
  - a coordinate isn't a whole number of 0 or more;
  - a line is missing, isn't a string, or has an unknown prefix;
  - the line counts don't match the hunk's lines;
  - it overlaps or comes before the previous hunk;
  - it runs past the end of the input;
  - its `newStart` doesn't match the shift caused by earlier hunks;
  - any ' ' or '-' line doesn't exactly match the original.
- Every error message contains `hunk <i> does not apply: <reason>`.
- A hunk with an empty `lines` array is rejected as malformed. The contract doesn't cover this case either.

**Temporary test results**
- **Random cases:** 20,000 with up to 12 lines each, drawn from a small repetitive set of lines that includes `''`, `' x'`, `'-y'` and `'+z'`. For each case:
  - the edit script rebuilt both inputs;
  - the number of edits matched what a full table-based LCS predicts;
  - no insert came directly before a delete;
  - repeated calls gave the same result;
  - neither input was changed;
  - applying the hunks to the original gave back the new lines exactly, for context 0–3.
- **Insert/delete at the top:** gave `oldStart: 0` and `newStart: 0` respectively.
- **Speed on 20,000 lines** (repetitive content):
  - a few scattered edits: 2 ms;
  - building hunks and applying them back: 3 ms;
  - identical inputs: 1 ms;
  - two completely different 5,000-line files: 136 ms.
- **Rejection checks:** hunks out of order, a wrong `newStart`, wrong counts, a bad prefix and a content mismatch each threw the expected `hunk N does not apply` message. An empty hunk list returned a new copy of the input.
